# M39 (PLAN_BROWSER_PERF_SECURITY.md §2.10, D49) — isolated POST-uncontended rung against the real
# dogfood target: `POST /cart/items`, a static (non-interpolated, no capture) body against a
# per-user cart row (no shared row lock across VUs — unlike the acceptance scenario's checkout,
# every VU only ever touches its own cart), matching M34's own "POST /cart/items only, static body,
# uncontended" row. `productId` below is the real "Load Test Widget" product's id — hardcoded rather
# than captured so the load run itself pays for zero capture/interpolation overhead, isolating the
# raw POST-with-body cost the same way M34's original row did.
#
# That id is now **pinned in the seed** (`LOAD_HOT_PRODUCT_ID`, apiV2/src/load-admin/
# load-target.constants.ts) and no longer needs re-resolving after a reseed. It used to be whatever
# UUID Postgres generated, which meant every fresh `node cli.mjs start` silently invalidated this
# fixture and its k6/Artillery counterparts. Found live 2026-08-05, several reseeds stale: 65,119
# iterations, 100 % error rate — and until the `threshold` line below existed, a green PASS and exit
# 0. Change this id in one place and all four (constant, tflw, k6, Artillery) must move together.
#
# Same ramp shape as checkout-burst.tflw for apples-to-apples comparison against the contended rung.

test "M39 dogfood POST-uncontended" as load
  ramp to 60 users over 20s
  api POST /cart/items body { productId: "10ad7e57-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", quantity: 1 }
  expect status equals 201
  # `TF033` (tflw M60): a workload-bearing test's verdict comes only from its thresholds, so one is
  # required. This rung measures rather than gates, so the bound is deliberately loose — but not
  # vacuous: each VU writes only its own cart row, so an error here means a stale fixture (the
  # hardcoded `productId` after a reseed) or a genuinely failing server, never contention. See
  # perf/README.md for how this ladder is run and checked.
  threshold error rate is less than 1%
